Pattern matching for switch, with guards.

The type pattern from the last section becomes genuinely powerful when you put it inside a switch. Pattern matching for switch, which is final in Java twenty-one, lets each case match on a type, bind a variable, and — with a guard — add a condition. Branching over "which kind of thing is this?" collapses from an instanceof ladder into one clean, checkable construct.

Start with the basic form: a case on a type pattern. Imagine a describe method that switches on an object. One case says: case Integer i, arrow, "int" plus i. Another: case String s, arrow, "string of length" plus s dot length. Another handles an int array. And a default catches anything else. Each case tests the runtime type and binds the value, already cast. This is the instanceof ladder rewritten as a single expression that returns a value.

But a type match is often not specific enough. You want "a string, but only if it's blank." For that, you add a guarded pattern, using the keyword when, followed by a boolean. So: case String s when s is blank, arrow, "blank string". Then, below it, case String s, arrow, "string:" plus s, catching all the other strings. And you can do the same with integers — case Integer i when i is less than zero, for "negative", then a plain Integer case for the rest. Order matters with guards. Cases are tried top to bottom, so you put the more specific, guarded case before the general one. And the guard only runs after the type has matched, so s is already a string by the time s-is-blank is evaluated.

Now, null. Historically, a switch threw a null-pointer exception if the value was null. A pattern switch lets you handle it explicitly, with a case null. And you can even fold it in with the default, by writing case null, default. If you write no case null, the classic null-pointer-on-null behaviour still applies — so handle null deliberately whenever the value might be one.

Finally, the real payoff: exhaustiveness with sealed types. Put a pattern switch together with a sealed type, and the compiler can prove your switch covers every case — so you need no default. Switch on a sealed shape, handle circle, square, and triangle, and you're done. No default. And if a pentagon later joins the sealed family, this switch stops compiling, until you handle it. So resist the urge to add a default "just in case" over a sealed type — because that default is exactly what silences the helpful error. A sealed family, plus a pattern switch, is the idiomatic modern replacement for the old visitor pattern and the type-code ladder. And it gets sharper still when the patterns reach inside the records themselves — which is destructuring, coming up next.
